  The GNU General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
 software and other kinds of works.

  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
 to take away your freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast,
 the GNU General Public License is intended to guarantee your freedom to
 share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains free
 software for all its users.  We, the Free Software Foundation, use the
 GNU General Public License for most of our software; it applies also to
 any other work released this way by us.  The GNU General Public License
 is designed to make sure that when you change a program, you are not
 required to give up the freedom to share and change the changes.

  When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not
 price.  Our General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you
 have the freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for
 this service if you wish), that you receive source code or can get it
 in a way you are free to change it, and that you know you can do these
 things.

  To protect your rights, we need to prevent others from denying you
 these rights or asking you to surrender the rights.  The GPL says you
 have the freedom to distribute copies of the original program, and the
 freedom to modify and share the changes, and the freedom to share and
 change versions of a program.

  You must license the entire work as a whole under this License to
 anyone who comes to possess a copy.  This License will therefore
 apply to the whole modified work.  If you cannot do this, you must
 not distribute the modified work.

  A "contributor" is a copyright holder who authorizes use under this
 License.  The "contributor's statement" is the statement of the
 contributor's identity and the license of the work.

  "Convey" a work means any kind of propagation that enables other
 parties to make or receive copies.  Mere interaction with a user
 through a computer network does not count as conveying.

  An interactive user interface displays "Appropriate Legal Notices"
 to the extent that it includes a convenient and prominently visible
 feature that (1) displays an appropriate copyright notice, and (2)
 tells the user that there is no warranty for the work (except to the
 extent that warranties are implied), and that the user has the right
 to the source code of the work that has been modified.

  For a physical object, "modification" means making a different
 object.  For a work of software or documentation, "modification"
 means making a different work or translating it into a different
 language.  The modified work must be licensed under the GPL.

  A "license" is a permission granted by the copyright holder for
 the user to use the work.  The "license of a work" is the license
 that applies to the work.

  "Object code" means any non-source form of a work.

  "Source code" means the preferred form of the work for making
 modifications to it.

  "Standard Interface" means an interface that either is an
 interface between two systems of standard production, or is an
 interface that is a standard interface used with particular
 equipment.

  "System Libraries" of an executable work are the functions and
 data structures that are part of the standard operations of the
 operating system, or that provide standard services.

  "You" means the individual or organization exercising the
 permissions granted by this license.  "Your" refers to the
 permissions you exercise.  "Your license" means the license that
 applies to your copy of the work.  "Your licensor" means the
 copyright holder who authorizes you.

  "Supplementary information" is information that you provide or
 require.

